---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import ForFramework from '@/components/ForFramework.astro';
import Code from '@/components/Code.astro';
import MotionStage from '@/components/motion/MotionStage.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import Presets from '@/components/motion/scenes/Presets.astro';
import { PresetsControls } from '@/components/motion/scenes/PresetsControls';
import { chipClass } from '@/components/motionDemo';

const verbs = [
  ['appear', 'Enters through its keyframes, and leaves backward.', 'A presence: it is inserted or shown, and its exit plays when it is hidden. See Show and hide.'],
  ['disappear', 'The reverse of appear: the exit of a presence.', 'See Show and hide.'],
  ['emphasize', 'Plays its keyframes forward on each replay.', 'Replays on each change of data-motion-state, or with play(). See Draw attention.'],
  ['layout', 'Moves the element to its new place and size when the layout around it changes.', 'Keyframes are not used: see Move to a new place.'],
  ['reveal', 'Plays when the element scrolls into view.', 'See Reveal on scroll.'],
  ['scrub', 'Follows the scroll position.', 'See Link to the scroll.'],
  ['loop', 'Plays forever, and can be paused.', 'See Loop, and pause it.'],
];
const presets = [
  ['fade', 'appear', 'Fades in.'],
  ['slide', 'appear', 'Fades in while rising 24 px.'],
  ['cascade', 'appear', 'A list entering together: each item fades in while rising 24 px, one step after the previous one (stagger normal).'],
  ['scale', 'appear', 'Fades in while growing from 80 %.'],
  ['draw', 'appear', 'Draws a path or a basic shape from nothing to its whole stroke (see SVG).'],
  ['press', 'emphasize', 'Dips to 96 % and returns.'],
  ['shake', 'emphasize', 'Shakes sideways by 6 px, then settles, to signal a refused action.'],
  ['reflow', 'layout', 'Moves to the new place and size of the element when the layout changes.'],
  ['rise', 'reveal', 'Fades in while rising 24 px, when it enters the view (stagger normal).'],
  ['parallax', 'scrub', 'Drifts from 48 px below its place to 48 px above it, across the viewport.'],
  ['rotate', 'loop', 'Turns a full turn in one second. The user can pause it.'],
  ['breathe', 'loop', 'Swells to 104 % and back in one second. The user can pause it.'],
  ['blink', 'loop', 'One dim flash each second. The user can pause it.'],
];

const defineCardEnter = `import { defineMotion } from '@udixio/motion';

// A name, a verb and keyframes are enough: the timing is optional.
defineMotion('card-enter', {
  verb: 'appear',
  keyframes: [
    { opacity: 0, y: 24 },
    { opacity: 1, y: 0 },
  ],
  timing: 'spring.default.spatial',
});`;

const presetImports = `// Defines every preset, so that data-motion can name each of them.
import '@udixio/motion/presets';

// Or import only the presets you use, by name, as references.
import { fade, slide, reflow } from '@udixio/motion/presets';`;
---
<MotionPage
  slug="presets"
  title="Motion: presets and verbs"
  description="The verbs of @udixio/motion, the defineMotion function, and the presets that are defined by importing @udixio/motion/presets."
  frameworkSwitch
>
  <ForFramework as="span" slot="summary">
    <Fragment slot="html">A preset is a motion that is already defined: put its name in <code>data-motion</code> and it plays. Define your own with <code>defineMotion()</code>.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="react">A preset is a motion that is already defined: render an element as a <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code> with its name. Define your own with <code>defineMotion()</code>.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="angular">A preset is a motion that is already defined: put its name in <code>udxMotion</code>. Define your own with <code>defineMotion()</code>.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="svelte">A preset is a motion that is already defined: attach <code>motion()</code> with its name. Define your own with <code>defineMotion()</code>.</Fragment>
  </ForFramework>

  <MotionStage scene="presets" label="Live example: Presets and verbs" height={[856, 2096]}>
    <Fragment slot="controls"><PresetsControls client:visible /></Fragment>
    <Presets />
    <Fragment slot="readout"><span class={`${chipClass} tracking-normal`} data-presets-readout>Press a preset to replay it.</span></Fragment>
  </MotionStage>

  <h2>Define a motion of your own</h2>
  <p>
    Define a motion with <code>defineMotion(name, spec)</code>, once, before an element uses it. The name is kebab-case, such as
    <code>card-enter</code>. Reserved names, such as <code>enter</code> or <code>exit</code>, are refused.
  </p>
  <Code data-code={defineCardEnter} data-language="ts" />
  <p>The spec has three fields:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>verb</code> is one of the seven verbs below.</li>
    <li>
      <code>keyframes</code> are the visual states, from the first to the last. Their fields are <code>opacity</code>, <code>x</code>,
      <code>y</code>, <code>scale</code>, <code>scaleX</code>, <code>scaleY</code> and <code>rotate</code>. <code>scale</code> scales
      both axes. A frame sets either <code>scale</code>, or <code>scaleX</code> and <code>scaleY</code>: development refuses a frame
      that sets both. <code>draw</code> and <code>along</code> are for shapes and paths, see <a href="/motion/svg">Draw or morph SVG</a>.
      A layout motion takes no keyframes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>timing</code> is optional: a spring token, such as <code>spring.fast.spatial</code>, or a duration and an easing, as in
      <code>&#123; duration: 'duration.medium2', easing: 'easing.standard' &#125;</code>. It applies to every field of the motion.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>Defining a name again with the same spec returns the existing motion. Defining it with a different spec throws.</p>

  <h2>Choose a verb</h2>
  <p>The verb says what the motion does. There are seven.</p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Verb</th><th>What it does</th><th>Where it is documented</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {verbs.map(([verb, does, where]) => <tr><td><code>{verb}</code></td><td>{does}</td><td>{where}</td></tr>)}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Use a preset without defining it</h2>
  <p>
    Importing <code>@udixio/motion/presets</code> defines the motions below, so that you can name them in an element. You can also import the ones
    you use, by name, as references.
  </p>
  <Code data-code={presetImports} data-language="ts" />
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Preset</th><th>Verb</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {presets.map(([name, verb, effect]) => <tr><td><code>{name}</code></td><td><code>{verb}</code></td><td>{effect}</td></tr>)}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The values of the presets are provisional and not calibrated yet. Use <code>cascade</code> for a list that enters together, and
    <code>rise</code> for elements that enter as they scroll into view: see <a href="/motion/lists">Make a list appear</a> and
    <a href="/motion/reveal">Reveal on scroll</a>. <code>parallax</code> is the subject of
    <a href="/motion/scrub">Link to the scroll</a>, and the loops are on <a href="/motion/loop">Loop, and pause it</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Name a preset in your framework</h2>
  <p>The example shows a card that fades in and out, and a Save button that presses.</p>
  <ForFramework>
    <Fragment slot="html">
      <p>Put <code>data-motion</code> on the element, with the name of the preset: <code>data-motion="fade"</code> fades it in, and out when you hide it.</p>
      <p>For an emphasis, change <code>data-motion-state</code> each time the element must replay: <code>data-motion="press"</code> plays on each change.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <p>Render the element as a <code>&lt;Motion name="fade"&gt;</code> inside a <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code>: the element fades out before it leaves.</p>
      <p>For an emphasis, call <code>play()</code> from <code>useMotion('press')</code>. Put its <code>ref</code> on a plain wrapper around the element it presses.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="angular">
      <p>Put <code>udxMotion="fade"</code> on the element, and show it with <code>&#64;if</code>.</p>
      <p>For an emphasis, bind <code>[udxMotionState]</code> to a value: each change of the value replays the emphasis.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="svelte">
      <p>Attach <code>&#123;&#64;attach motion('fade')&#125;</code>, and add <code>transition:presence</code> to the element inside an <code>&#123;#if&#125;</code> block.</p>
      <p>For an emphasis, pass <code>state</code> in the options of <code>motion()</code>: each change of the value replays the emphasis.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </ForFramework>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-presets" live={false} />

  <h2>Timing and tokens</h2>
  <p>
    The fade and slide presets use the default springs: spatial for the movement, and effects for the fade. The rotate preset turns
    over the extra long linear curve. The Pause loops control in the stage holds the three loops that have one.
  </p>
  <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.default.spatial', 'duration.extraLong4', 'easing.linear']} />

  <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
  <p>
    A preset follows the reduced-motion policy of its verb: an entry or an exit becomes a short fade, an emphasis applies its end
    frame, a reveal shows at once, a scrub stays still and a loop does not play. See <a href="/motion/accessibility">Reduced motion</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If you name a preset without importing <code>@udixio/motion/presets</code>, nothing plays: import it, or define the name yourself.</li>
    <li>If you need a variant of a preset, define a new name for it: a name that is already defined with a different spec throws.</li>
    <li>If a definition is invalid, development reports it when you define it. A production build trusts the definition, so check the page in development first.</li>
  </ul>
</MotionPage>
